; expect 42
; Two consecutive allocations of the same size are adjacent: the bump
; allocator's fast path advances by the (16-byte aligned) request, so
; the second block begins exactly 64 bytes after the first.
;
; This is a REGRESSION TEST FOR AN INLINE-ASM CLOBBER, not for the
; allocator's arithmetic. The arm64 syscall asm listed the argument
; registers as inputs and did not declare them clobbered, but Darwin's
; `svc` destroys them - probed, x1 comes back 0. At -O1 and above LLVM
; therefore believed `%chunk` was still in x1 after the `mmap`, set
; `@__axiom_bump_end` to `addr + 0`, and every allocation failed the
; fast path and mapped a fresh megabyte: consecutive blocks came back
; 1 MiB apart. At -O0 the same IR is correct, because LLVM spills and
; reloads across the asm.
;
; The case is written to run under the conformance gate's own `llc`
; invocation, which does NOT pass -O0 - so this fails before the fix
; and passes after. A test that only ran at -O0 could not see it.
(import Mem)
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (let ((a (memAlloc 64))
        (b (memAlloc 64)))
    (if (== (- b a) 64) 42 1)))
